The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not merely blow; it screamed. It tore at the stone walls of the lighthouse, a constant, rhythmic reminder that Elias Thorne was the last human voice in a world of gray waves and white foam. For thirty years, Elias had been the keeper, a man who had traded the noise of the mainland for the singular, deafening silence of the sea. His life was a metronome: wind the clock, polish the lens, check the oil, sleep, repeat.

Then, on a Tuesday so gray it felt like the sky had forgotten how to be blue, the mail boat arrived.

Elias stood on the dock, his boots slipping on the wet planks, as the small vessel cut through the chop. The mailman, a young man with a face like a shield against the storm, handed over a single envelope. It was thick, cream-colored, and smelled faintly of lavender and old paper. There was no return address, only Elias’s name, written in a script so elegant it looked like it had been carved by a quill.

Elias brought the letter inside, his hands trembling slightly. He had not received a letter in years. The world had moved on to digital
